>>
endobj
359 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (4.4 Human Oversight)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
360 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (Maintaining sufficient internal documentation, traceability logs, and oversight checkpoints can support the investigation of AI Systems. It is recommended that reviewers are granted the authority to reverse, correct, or clarify outcomes where justified.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
361 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (The form of human oversight may vary depending on an organisation's size, resources, and risk profile. For example, larger organisations may establish dedicated review functions, while smaller enterprises may allocate these responsibilities to existing personnel. )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
362 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (4.5 Documentation and Analysis)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
363 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (Organisations are encouraged to record redress cases and outcomes to identify risks and improve model design, oversight, and communication. Periodic analysis of redress trends should inform broader AI risk management and accountability reviews, ensuring that lessons inform updates to training data, governance controls, and user communication.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
