372 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 759 0 R
/E (Operational Readiness: Organisations are encouraged to translate ethical values into "Operational Drivers", such as robust data governance, ensuring fairness, and maintaining meaningful human oversight.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
373 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 760 0 R
/E (Proactive Risk Mitigation: A risk-based approach allows developers to classify AI use cases of different risk levels, ensuring that safety measures are proportionate to the potential impact on society.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
374 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 761 0 R
/E (Shared Responsibility: Accountability is a collective duty. Organisations must define clear roles from the design phase to the eventual retirement of a system to maintain public trust.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
375 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 762 0 R
/E (Continuous Improvement: Through established redress processes and the analysis of feedback, the AI ecosystem can evolve, correcting biases and improving model design over time.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
376 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 131 0 R
/E (The adoption of this voluntary Code of Ethics represents a commitment to fostering a digital ecosystem where innovation and human values coexist. By using these pillars as considerations of AI governance in individual organisations, stakeholders can contribute to a future where AI serves as a transparent, inclusive, and reliable tool for national progress.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
